\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Set 4 - Instrumental Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
A Formal Discussion of IV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we have an outcome equation (i.e., second stage):
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\alpha+\theta T_{i}+x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

and a participation equation (i.e., first stage):
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T_{i}=\gamma+x_{i}^{\prime}\pi_{0}+v_{i}
\]

\end_inset

We are concerned that 
\begin_inset Formula $E[\epsilon_{i}v_{i}|x_{i}]\ne0$
\end_inset

.
 The path diagram version of the endogeneity problem: Treatment 
\begin_inset Formula $(T)$
\end_inset

 affects outcomes 
\begin_inset Formula $(y)$
\end_inset

, but the error term 
\begin_inset Formula $(\epsilon)$
\end_inset

 in the outcome equation influences both 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 The motivation behind the instrumental variables method is to isolate the
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

as-good-as-random
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation in a treatment variable.
 The method is used to address the problem of selection on unobservables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The IV solution is to find another variable (or set of variables) that belongs
 in the participation equation, but not the outcome equation.
 Let us denote this set of variables as 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we use the variation that these variables produce.
 Rewriting the participation equation, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T_{i}=\gamma+x_{i}^{\prime}\pi_{0}+z_{i}^{\prime}\pi_{1}+v_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}:$
\end_inset

 observables that belong in the outcome equation, i.e., exogeneous covariates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}:$
\end_inset

 observables that belong in the participation equation, but not the outcome
 equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}:$
\end_inset

 unobservables of the participation equation, some of which may also belong
 in the outcome equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}:$
\end_inset

 endogenous variable, which is determined within the system
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For IV estimation to work, we need
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A valid, or nontrivial, first stage: 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{1}\neq0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A valid exclusion restriction: 
\begin_inset Formula $E[z_{i}\cdot\epsilon_{i}|x_{i}]=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
The Ideal Instrument
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An ideal instrument is as good as randomlly assigned.
 Also, the instrument should have no effect on outcomes other than through
 the first-stage.
 Where can we find an instrumental variable? Good instruments usually come
 from institutional knowledge and the mechanisms about the processes that
 determine the variable of interest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Identifying Assumption: 
\series default
\emph default
An IV 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is not related to any other variable that may affect 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is only related to 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 through its affect on 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Intuition Behind IV Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose the instrument increases the endogenous variable (ex: schooling)
 by 0.2 and the outcome variable (ex: earnings) by 400.
 If the instrument is valid, then it is correct to say that an increase
 in earnings is a consequence of the increase in education.
 To elaborate, an increase in eduction causes an increase in earnings, i.e.,
 a one unit increase in education causes a 2000 unit increase in earnings.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Earn}_{i}=\alpha+\beta\cdot\mbox{Educ}_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In practice, the IV method is about estimating:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The effect of the instrument on the outcome (reduced-form)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The effect of the instrument on the endogenous variable (first-stage)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Intuitive Derivation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the following equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=T_{i}\beta+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}$
\end_inset

 is endogenous.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 be a valid instrument for 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We can formulate the reduced-form and first-stage equations, respectively:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{i} & = & z_{i}\pi_{1}+u_{i}\\
T_{i} & = & z_{i}\pi_{2}+e_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Then, we obtain an estimate for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta=\frac{\pi_{1}}{\pi_{2}}=\frac{\nicefrac{\mbox{Cov}(y_{i},z_{i})}{\mbox{Var}(z_{i})}}{\nicefrac{\mbox{Cov}(T_{i},z_{i})}{\mbox{Var}(z_{i})}}=\frac{\mbox{Cov}(y_{i},z_{i})}{\mbox{Cov}(T_{i},z_{i})}
\]

\end_inset

which is just the reduced-form estimate divided by the first-stage estimate.
 Note that the IV estimand is predicated on the notion that the first-stage
 is not zero.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
If the first stage is only marginally significantlly different from zero,
 the resulting IV estimates are unlikely to be informative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can also formulate a more direct derivation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{Cov}(y_{i},z_{i}) & = & \mbox{Cov}(T_{i}\beta+\epsilon_{i},z_{i})\\
 & = & \mbox{Cov}(T_{i}\beta+\epsilon_{i},z_{i})+Cov(\epsilon_{i},z_{i})\\
 & = & \beta\mbox{Cov}(T_{i},z_{i})+0\ \mbox{(by excludability assumption)}\\
\beta & = & \frac{\mbox{Cov}(y_{i},z_{i})}{\mbox{Cov}(T_{i},z_{i})}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
When Variables Are Correlated With the Instrument and Outcome
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the variable that is correlated with the instrument is unobservable,
 then the exclusion restriction does not hold.
 If the variable that is correlated with the instrument is observed 
\begin_inset Formula $(x)$
\end_inset

, then we need to make sure the estimated effects of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 aren't confounded by 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 We want to use the part of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 that is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 So, we use 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{z}_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{z}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the residual from a regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta_{IV}=\frac{\mbox{Cov}(y_{i},\tilde{z}_{i})}{\mbox{Cov}(T_{i},\tilde{z}_{i})}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As a matter of practice, when we believe exogenous covariates belong in
 the outcome equation, we include them in the first-stage regression.
 By construction, this ensures that we only use the variation in 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 that is orthogonal to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 This is a two-stage least squares (2SLS) estimate.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
The standard errors from the second-stage are incorrect (too small), so
 we will over reject the true estimate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Alternative Derivation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{IV}$
\end_inset

 (using matrix notation)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the following equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=X\beta+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times k$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $k\times1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times1$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose one variable in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is endogenous.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 as the exogenous components and the instrument.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 is also 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times k$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Z^{\prime}y & = & Z^{\prime}X\beta+Z^{\prime}\epsilon\\
\beta & = & \left(Z^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}Z^{\prime}y+\left(Z^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}Z^{\prime}\epsilon\\
\hat{\beta}_{IV} & = & \left(Z^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}Z^{\prime}y
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that the estimator is biased,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[\hat{\beta}_{IV}] & = & E\left[\left(Z^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}Z^{\prime}y\right]\\
 & = & E\left[\left(Z^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}Z^{\prime}X\beta+\left(Z^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}Z^{\prime}\epsilon\right]\\
 & = & \beta+E\left[(Z^{\prime}X)^{-1}Z^{\prime}\epsilon\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

According to Cameron and Trivedi (page 108), there is no simple way to obtain
 
\begin_inset Formula $E[\hat{\beta}_{IV}]$
\end_inset

.
 The expectation operator does not pass through the non-linear function.
 However, the estimator is still consistent, and consistency is easily demonstra
ted
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{IV} & = & \beta+(Z^{\prime}X)^{-1}Z^{\prime}\epsilon\\
\mbox{p\lim}\ \hat{\beta}_{IV} & = & \beta+\mbox{p\lim}\ \left(N^{-1}Z^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}\cdot\mbox{p\lim}\ N^{-1}Z^{\prime}\epsilon\\
 & = & \beta+\left[E(Z^{\prime}X)\right]^{-1}\cdot E(Z^{\prime}\epsilon)\\
 & = & \beta\ \ (\mbox{since}\ E(Z^{\prime}\epsilon)=0)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The 2SLS Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, let us consider the more general setting.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{dim}(Z)=r$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{dim}(X)=k$
\end_inset

.
 The model is just-identified if 
\begin_inset Formula $r=k$
\end_inset

, it is over-identified if 
\begin_inset Formula $r>k$
\end_inset

, and it is under-identified if 
\begin_inset Formula $r<k$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times k$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times r$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $r\geq k$
\end_inset

.
 The general estimator we obatain is the 2SLS estimator:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}=(\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X})^{-1}\hat{X}^{\prime}y
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}=Z\hat{\Pi}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Pi}$
\end_inset

 is estimated from
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X=Z\Pi+v
\]

\end_inset

Hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{X}=Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}X=P_{Z}X
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{Z}$
\end_inset

 is symmetric and idempotent.
 Thus, the 2SLS estimator can be rewritten as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{2SLS} & = & (\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X})^{-1}\hat{X}^{\prime}y\\
 & = & \left[(P_{Z}X)^{\prime}(P_{Z}X)\right]^{-1}(P_{Z}X)^{\prime}y\\
 & = & \left(X^{\prime}P_{Z}^{\prime}P_{Z}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}P_{Z}^{\prime}y\\
 & = & \left(X^{\prime}P_{z}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}P_{z}y\\
 & = & \left[X^{\prime}Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}X\right]^{-1}X^{\prime}Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}y
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The bonus of this general approach is that we can use variation from multiple
 instruments, which improves efficiency.
 We could perform the estimation in two steps, but the standard errors are
 too small.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Asymptotic Distribution of the 2SLS Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can rewrite the 2SLS estimator as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}=\beta+\left[X^{\prime}Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}X\right]^{-1}\left[X^{\prime}Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}\epsilon\right]
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By the CLT, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}-\beta\right) & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array} & \left[E(X^{\prime}Z)\left[E(Z^{\prime}Z)\right]^{-1}E(Z^{\prime}X)\right]^{-1}\left[E(X^{\prime}Z)\left[E(Z^{\prime}Z)\right]^{-1}\right]\cdot N\left(0,E\left(Z^{\prime}\epsilon\epsilon^{\prime}Z\right)\right)\\
 & = & N\left(0,V_{R}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cdot N(0,B)=N(0,ABA^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{R}$
\end_inset

 is the robust variance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V_{R}=\left(X^{\prime}P_{Z}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}S(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}P_{Z}X\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

and it is estimated by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}_{R}$
\end_inset

, where the expectations in 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{R}$
\end_inset

 are replaced by sample averages.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{V}(\hat{\beta}_{2SLS})=\left(X^{\prime}P_{Z}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}S(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}P_{Z}X\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

With heteroskedasticity, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{S}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{\epsilon}_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\epsilon}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Angrist and Krueger (1992)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the following thought experiment: the combination of school entry
 cutoffs and compulsory schooling laws leads to variation in education predicted
 by quarter of birth (QOB).
 For example, a child must be six by January 1st to begin school, and the
 law requires attendance until 16.
 So, if a child was born between September to December, then he could start
 school at age 5; he would be in the 11th grade at age 16.
 If a child was born between January to March, then he would have to wait
 an extra year before attending school; he would be in the 10th grade at
 age 16.
 As a result, otherwise identical children get different education becase
 of these institutions.
 Thus, the authors use QOB as an instrument, which is available in the census
 data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
2SLS Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The authors could have simply used 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

born in quarter 1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as instrument.
 Alternatively, they could use QOB, or QOB interacted with cohort, where
 a cohort is associated with birth year.
 This method seems desirable for efficiency, and it is the approach employed
 by the authors.
 So, the preferred set of instruments is 
\begin_inset Formula $QOB\times YOB$
\end_inset

.
 Now, we have the participation equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{ijc}=X_{ijc}\Pi+\alpha_{c}+\alpha_{jc}+\epsilon_{ijc}
\]

\end_inset

and the outcome equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\log W_{ijc}=X_{ijc}\beta+\lambda_{c}+\rho E_{ijc}+u_{ijc}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $E_{ijc}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{ijc}$
\end_inset

 are the education and wage levels, respectively, for person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, born in quarter 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, and part of cohort 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{ijc}$
\end_inset

 contains individual characteristics, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{c}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{c}$
\end_inset

 are cohort fixed effects, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{jc}$
\end_inset

 is QOB and cohort fixed effects, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ijc}$
\end_inset

 is the error term.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Why did the authors include 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{c}$
\end_inset

? Well, earlier cohorts will tend to have less education, but they may have
 higher earnings because they are older, which could lead to bias.
 With cohort fixed effects, we are using the variation in QOB within cohorts,
 which fits the thought experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Identifying assumption without cohort fixed effects:
\series default
 The year and QOB interaction relates to earnings only through completed
 education.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Identifying assumption with cohort fixed effects:
\series default
 Conditional on being born in a given year, the year and QOB interaction
 is only related to earnings through completed education.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The authors could have also controlled for QOB, then the identifying variation
 changes would be based on the change in the relationship between QOB and
 education across cohorts.
 This would address the possibility that there may be systematic differences
 across those born in different quarters if these differences are constant
 over time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Above all, the authors were trying to utilize as much variation as possible.
 So, they use the interaction between state and QOB as additional instruments.
 They use the fact that the link between QOB and education is stronger in
 some states than others after controlling for state fixed effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Impacts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Contributed to the literature concerning weak instruments: 2SLS biased towards
 OLS with weak instruments, even with a large sample size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors used the variation predicted by the timing of births, but QOB
 is not random.
 There is seasonality in births, and it varies across socio-economic status.
 Yet, the exact DOB is arguably random.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What about estimating the effect of school starting ages on outcomes in
 school, such as first grade test scores?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A child who starts early will necessarily be younger when they take the
 test.
 So, we cannot separate the two.
 This is a fundamentally unidentified question.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
How about using school starting ages to get at the effects of attainment?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The exclusion restriction is not valid because school starting ages affects
 quality of education, such as peer group dynamics (which could have a direct
 effect on educational outcomes), as well as quantity.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
Additional Thoughts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There is another way to think about this paper.
 The effects of 
\begin_inset Formula $QOB\times YOB$
\end_inset

, which is the preferred set of instruments, on education will reflect differenc
es in:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Education across QOB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Education across YOB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The link between education and QOB across YOB.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Angrist and Krueger don't argue that YOB doesn't belong in the outcome equation,
 nor could they.
 So, it's critical that they control for YOB when they use the 
\begin_inset Formula $QOB\times YOB$
\end_inset

 instrument.
 Though, given that they have made the case that QOB does not belong in
 the outcome equation, one can see why they would not control for QOB even
 though they could.
 Moreover, recall that the whole reason they use the broader set of instruments
 was to try to improve efficiency, i.e., to use 
\series bold
more
\series default
 variation than they get with QOB alone (quarters 1 and 3, instead of just
 quarter 1).
 Controlling for QOB would throw out the variation, which would be bad for
 efficiency.
 However, if you believe QOB belongs in the outcome equation, then it would
 be the right thing to do.
 Of course, all of this discussion abstracts away from the weak instruments
 problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Wald Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Wald Estimator is a special case of IV.
 It can be obtained when we have a just identified model with no covariates,
 and the instrument 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable.
 So, we can get the IV estimate by comparing group means:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{Wald}=\frac{\bar{y}_{z=1}-\bar{y}_{z=0}}{\bar{T}_{z=1}-\bar{T}_{z=0}}=\frac{\nicefrac{dy}{dz}}{\nicefrac{dT}{dz}}
\]

\end_inset

The denominator 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

scales up
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the mean difference in outcomes to account for the fact that the instrument
 has an effect on treatment, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $T>1$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $T<1$
\end_inset

.
 Also, note that 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 signifies the offer of treatment, not if treatment is actually received.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Wald Estimator is useful for field experiments:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There is random assignment, so no covariates are needed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The compliance rate does not equal 100%, and often the controls 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

can get treatment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 serves as an instrument for treatment.
 The effect of the offer on outcomes, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{y}_{z=1}-\bar{y}_{z=0}
\]

\end_inset

is known as the 
\series bold
\emph on
intent-to-treat effect (ITT)
\series default
\emph default
.
 The effect of the offer on treatment, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{T}_{z=1}-\bar{T}_{z=0}
\]

\end_inset

is known as the 
\series bold
\emph on

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

take up rate
\series default
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 when we can exclude the control group from the treatment group.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}_{z=1}$
\end_inset

 is known as the 
\series bold
\emph on

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

complicance rate,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\series default
\emph default
 i.e., people who were offered treatment and chose to accept it.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}_{z=0}$
\end_inset

 represents the fraction of people who were not offered treatment, but still
 obtained treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The effect of treatment can be obtained as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\mbox{ITT}}{\mbox{Take Up Rate}}
\]

\end_inset

Note that the ITT may be the estimate that is policy relevant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Two Sample IV/2SLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Here, we use different samples to estimate the reduced-form and/or 1st-stage
 regressions.
 The outcome is in one data set, the endogenous variable is in another,
 and the instrument is in both.
 Though, if a variable belongs in the outcome equation, then both equations
 need it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For example, suppose we have the outcome and the instrument in data set
 1 and the endogenous variable and the instrument in data set 2.
 We wish to estimate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\alpha+\beta X_{1}+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

Even though we only have 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

 (the endogenous variable in data set 2), we obtain a predicted value for
 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{1}$
\end_inset

 from the first-stage regression
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X_{2}=Z_{2}\Pi+v
\]

\end_inset

Then, we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\theta}_{IV}=\left(\nicefrac{Z_{2}^{\prime}X}{n_{2}}\right)^{-1}\left(\nicefrac{Z_{1}^{\prime}y}{n_{1}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\theta}_{2SLS}=\left(\hat{X}_{1}^{\prime}\hat{X}_{1}\right)^{-1}\left(\hat{X}_{1}y\right)
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{X}_{1}=Z_{1}\left(Z_{2}^{\prime}Z_{2}\right)^{-1}Z_{2}^{\prime}X_{2}
\]

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}_{2SLS}$
\end_inset

 corrects for differences in the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 across the data sets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Examples:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Angrist and Krueger (JASA, 1992): 1960 census to estimate effect of QOB
 on school entry.
 1980 census to estimate the effect of QOB on completed education.
 Combining gives us effect of age at entry on attainment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Dee and Evans (JOLE, 2003): Shows that lowering minimum legal drinking age
 increases teen drinking.
 Shows that there is no effect of lowering minimum legal drinking age on
 educational attainment in another.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Lindo and Stoecker (forthcoming): Exact date of birth affects draft eligibility
 for Vietnam chorts.
 2000 Census: estimate the effect of draft eligibility on military service.
 Using inmate data and birth data to estimate effect of eligibility on incarcera
tion rates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Measurement Error (Classical)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we have classical measurement error (ME) in the outcome (i.e., on the LHS),
 then the estimates are still unbiased, but they are less precise.
 The estimator we obtain is inefficient because the standard errors are
 too big.
 However, classical ME in the regressor (i.e., on the RHS) causes the estimates
 to be biased towards zero, which is known as 
\series bold
\emph on
attenuation bias
\series default
\emph default
.
 IV is a solution to measurement error on the RHS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Intuition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the true model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\alpha+\beta x^{*}+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

Instead, we estimate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\alpha+\beta x+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x=x^{*}+u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is a random error term.
 So, what we are actually estimating is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\alpha+\beta(x^{*}+u)+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is a/an (variance) average of the effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}+u$
\end_inset

.
 Since the effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is zero, then 
\begin_inset Formula $|\hat{\beta}|<\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Alternatively, we can think of estimating the regression equation such
 that we have restricted the effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 to be the same as 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\alpha+\beta x^{*}+\beta u+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

But, in reality,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\alpha+\beta x^{*}+\gamma u+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Magnitude of Bias
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The magnitude of bias depends on the extent to which the variation in 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 reflects the variaiton in 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

versus 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{p}\lim\ \hat{\beta} & = & \frac{\sigma_{x^{*}}^{2}}{\sigma_{x^{*}}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}}\beta\\
 & = & v\beta
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is known as the reliability ratio, or the signal-to-total variance ratio.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Contamination Bias
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the following equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\beta_{2}x_{2}+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 is measured with error.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{1}$
\end_inset

 is attenuated (i.e., biased toward zero).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, holding constant the effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}x_{1}$
\end_inset

).
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 are correlated, then our ability to obtain an unbiased estiamte of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 hinges on our ability to obtain an unbiased estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{2}$
\end_inset

 could be biased upwards or downwards.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The solution to this problem is to find an IV for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and proceed as you would for any other IV application.
 The intuition is that the IV isolates the correctly-measured variation
 in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Two Solutions to Measurement Error Bias
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Method 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Find an instrument correlated with the variable measured with error that
 is otherwise uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 Any instrument that generates good variation will be useful here, i.e., a
 variable that does not affect measurement error or any other variable related
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Suppose we wish to estimate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\alpha+\beta x^{*}+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x=x^{*}+u$
\end_inset

.
 Classcical measurement error occurs when 
\begin_inset Formula $u\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 We need to find an instrument 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 that is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

, but not with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Policies that affect 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

, but not 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, are good choices.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{IV} & = & \frac{\mbox{Cov}(y,z)}{\mbox{Cov}(x,z)}\\
 & = & \frac{\mbox{Cov}(\alpha+\beta x^{*}+\epsilon,z)}{\mbox{Cov}(x^{*}+u,z)}\\
 & = & \beta\frac{\mbox{Cov}(x^{*},z)}{\mbox{Cov}(x^{*},z)}\\
 & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array} & \beta
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The instrument 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

, but it is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another common approach is to use some other noisy measure of the variable
 of interest.
 Suppose we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\alpha+\beta+x^{*}+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x=x^{*}+u$
\end_inset

, and an instrument 
\begin_inset Formula $z=x^{*}+v$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 are independent, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{IV} & = & \frac{\mbox{Cov}(y,z)}{\mbox{Cov}(x,z)}\\
 & = & \frac{\mbox{Cov}(\alpha+\beta x^{*}+\epsilon,x^{*}+v)}{\mbox{Cov}(x^{*}+u,x^{*}+v)}\\
 & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array} & \beta
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Here is another way to think about the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

IV fix
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to measurement error.
 The idea is that the predicted 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x},$
\end_inset

 from a regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is driven by the variation in 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, regressing 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}$
\end_inset

 will reflect the variation in 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Method 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Use estimates of the reliability ratio (or, equivalently, know 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(x^{*})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u)$
\end_inset

) to inflate the estimate appropriately.
 This method is touched upon in the following papers: Ashenfe'ter and Krueger
 (1994); Card (1996); Krashinsky (2004).
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{IV}=[X^{\prime}X-N\Sigma_{uu}]^{-1}X^{\prime}y
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{uu}$
\end_inset

 is the variance-covariance matrix for the measurement error in 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{p}\lim\ \hat{\beta}_{IV} & = & \mbox{p}\lim\ [N^{-1}X^{\prime}X-\Sigma_{uu}]^{-1}\cdot\mbox{p}\lim\ N^{-1}X^{\prime}y\\
 & = & \mbox{p}\lim\ [N^{-1}(X^{*}+u)^{\prime}(X^{*}+u)-\Sigma_{uu}]^{-1}N^{-1}(X^{*}+u)^{\prime}\left[\left(X^{*}+u\right)\beta+\epsilon\right]\\
 & = & \left[\Sigma_{X^{*}X^{*}}+\Sigma_{uu}-\Sigma_{uu}\right]\Sigma_{X^{*}X^{*}}\beta\\
 & = & \beta
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

because 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{*}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Possible Problems With IV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
I) Finite Sample Bias
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Recall that the IV and 2SLS estimators are consistent, but they are not
 unbiased.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\hat{\beta}_{IV}\right]=\beta+E\left[(Z^{\prime}X)^{-1}Z^{\prime}\epsilon\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}\right]=\beta+E\left[(\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X})^{-1}\hat{X}^{\prime}\epsilon\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The bias term only goes to zero as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We will never be able to obtain an infinite sample, so how big is the bias?
 Clearly, it depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X}$
\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(\hat{X})$
\end_inset

.
 If the instrument generates a large amount of variation in 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(\hat{X})$
\end_inset

 is large, and the bias is small.
 If the instrument generates almost no variation in 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Pi}=0$
\end_inset

), then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(\hat{X})=0$
\end_inset

, and the estimator does not exist.
 Even if 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi=0$
\end_inset

, it is unlikely that we will find 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Pi}=0$
\end_inset

.
 The estimates derived from this 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Pi}$
\end_inset

 are not to be trusted because the first-stage is spurious.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The bias tends to be exacerbated as (weak) instruments are added.
 Consider the extreme case where the number of instruments equals the number
 of observations.
 Then, we fit the data perfectly such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X}=X^{\prime}X$
\end_inset

.
 This happens because we are getting 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Pi}$
\end_inset

, not 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

.
 So, the bias is the same as OLS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The overall punchline: a strong first-stage is necessary to obtain approximately
 unbiased estimates.
 We only want to add instruments that really give us additional variation.
 Do not use weak instruments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Formal Derivation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the following equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=X\beta+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 is our instrument.
 The first-stage regression is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X=Z\Pi+v
\]

\end_inset

Remember, the expected bias is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[\mbox{bias}] & = & E\left[(X^{\prime}P_{Z}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}P_{Z}\epsilon\right]\\
 & = & E\left[(X^{\prime}P_{Z}X)^{-1}(Z\Pi+v)^{\prime}P_{Z}\epsilon\right]\\
 & = & E\left[(X^{\prime}P_{Z}X)^{-1}\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}\epsilon+(X^{\prime}P_{Z}X)^{-1}v^{\prime}P_{Z}\epsilon\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The expecation of these ratios is approximately equal to the ratio of expectatio
ns.
 For further information, read about group asymptotics.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[\mbox{bias}]\approx(E[X^{\prime}P_{Z}X])^{-1}E[\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}\epsilon]+(E[X^{\prime}P_{Z}X])^{-1}E[v^{\prime}P_{z}\epsilon]
\]

\end_inset

The first term drops out since 
\begin_inset Formula $E[\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}\epsilon]=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[\mbox{bias}] & \approx & (E[X^{\prime}P_{Z}X])^{-1}E[v^{\prime}P_{Z}\epsilon]\\
 & \approx & (E[(Z\Pi+v)^{\prime}P_{Z}(Z\Pi+v)])^{-1}E[v^{\prime}P_{Z}\epsilon]\\
 & \approx & (E[(\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}+v^{\prime})P_{z}(Z\Pi+v)])^{-1}E[v^{\prime}P_{z}\epsilon]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We can ignore the cross products since 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}v\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
 Hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[\mbox{bias}] & \approx & (E[(\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}P_{Z}Z\Pi+v^{\prime}P_{Z}v)])^{-1}E[v^{\prime}P_{Z}\epsilon]\\
 & \approx & (E[\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}Z\Pi]+E[v^{\prime}P_{Z}v])^{-1}E[v^{\prime}P_{Z}\epsilon]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[v^{\prime}P_{Z}v]\rightarrow\sigma_{v}^{2}Q
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[v^{\prime}P_{z}\epsilon]\rightarrow\sigma_{v\epsilon}Q
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=\mbox{rank}(Z)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[\mbox{bias}]\approx\frac{\sigma_{v\epsilon}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\left[\frac{\nicefrac{E[\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}Z\Pi]}{Q}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}+1\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

The population F-stat for join significance on the first-stage is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F=\frac{\nicefrac{E[\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}Z\Pi]}{Q}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\]

\end_inset

Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[\mbox{bias}]\approx\frac{\sigma_{v\epsilon}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\frac{1}{F+1}
\]

\end_inset

where we note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\sigma_{v\epsilon}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\]

\end_inset

is the approximate bias of OLS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we can see the problem caused by adding weak instruments.
 
\begin_inset Formula $E[\Pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}Z\Pi]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

 stay the same, but 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 increases.
 Consequently, the F-statistic becomes smaller.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[\mbox{bias}]\rightarrow\frac{\sigma_{v\epsilon}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bound, Jaeger, and Baker (1995) brought the problem of weak instruments
 to the attention of researchers.
 Surprisingly, they found that finite sample bias was present even with
 a huge census sample because of the use of weak instruments.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, how strong does the first-stage need to be? According to Staiger and
 Stock (1997), an F-stat testing the joint significance of the instruments
 that is greater than 10 should be good if you are willing to accept bias
 that is no more than 10% of bias from OLS.
 If you are willing to accept bias that is no more than 20% of bias from
 OLS, then the F-state should be greater than 6.5.
 The first-stage regression must include all of the covariates that belong
 in the outcome equation in addition to the instruments because this is
 the regression that provides the appropriate 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

 that produces the 2SLS estimator.
 Yet, the F-test we are interested in is 
\series bold
\emph on
not 
\series default
\emph default
the F-test for the joint significance of all of the variables in the first-stage
 (instruments and covariates).
 It is the F-test for the joint significance of the instruments in the first-sta
ge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Limited Information Maximum Likelihood
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This estimation method has better small sample properties than 2SLS when
 there is a weak first-stage.
 It is the joint estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=X\beta+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X=Z\Pi+v
\]

\end_inset

in which we assume homoskedastic, normal errors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
II) Invalid Exclusion Restriction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One can never be certain that the exclusion restriction holds.
 Instead, you must make the case that it is likely to hold.
 There are two issues to consider:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Are you sure the instrument does not affect outcomes through any other channel?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What factors affect the instrument? Are you sure they are not also related
 to the outcome?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Institutional knowledge plays a huge role here.
 To be credible, you must demonstrate that you have looked as hard as possible.
 Is there evidence that the exclusion restriction does not hold? There are
 two approaches to help address this concern.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Is the Instrument Related to Observables?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can test whether or not the instrument is related to observables we know
 to be related to outcomes.
 If the answer is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

yes,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then there is concern that the instrument may also be related to unobservables
 that are related to outcomes, which would violate the exclusion restriction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Test of Overidentifying Restrictions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To explain these tests, it helps to understand 2SLS as a form of GMM.
 With methods of moments (MM), we take moment conditions, sub-sample analogues,
 and the solve.
 For example, using the OLS moment conditions,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left[x_{i}u_{i}\right] & = & 0\\
E\left[x_{i}(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right] & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

we can obtain the OLS estimator from the corresponding sub-sample analogues
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{1}{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)=0\\
\implies\hat{\beta}_{MM}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}y_{i}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

We can use the same approach to obtain the IV estimator,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
E[z_{i}u_{i}]=0\\
\implies\hat{\beta}_{MM}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}y_{i}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If there are more moments then paraments, then there is no unique solution.
 Instead, we use GMM estimation, which sets the sample moments as close
 to zero as possible using quadratic loss.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
E[Z^{\prime}u]=0\\
\mbox{min}\ Q(\beta)=\left[Z^{\prime}(y-X\beta)\right]^{\prime}W\left[Z^{\prime}(y-X\beta)\right]\\
\implies\hat{\beta}_{GMM}=\left(X^{\prime}ZWZ^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}ZWZ^{\prime}y
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Thus, the 2SLS estimator is a GMM estimator with 
\begin_inset Formula $W=(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
2SLS versus Efficient GMM
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Researches tend to strongly favor 2SLS as opposed to a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

two-step GMM
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 approach where the optimal weighting matrix is estimated.
 Cameron and Trivedi (pg.
 177) explains that it all comes down to finite sample bias.
 Asymptotically, we prefer GMM.
 In finite samples, not so much.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Test of Overidentifying Restrictions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The GMM estimator is based on the population specified moment conditions.
 Are they all valid? Is it possible that some of them are invalid? Can we
 test their validity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We cannot test the validity of all moments because we need at least the
 minimum nmber of just-identifying moment conditions for identifiability
 of parameters.
 When there are more moment conditions than needed to determine parameters,
 we can test whether extra moment conditions are valid.
 The null and alternative hypotheses are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
H_{0}:E[Z^{\prime}u]=0\\
H_{a}:H_{0}\ \mbox{is false}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

The null hypothesis of the test says that all the moment conditions are
 valid.
 The alternative hypothesis says that at least one of the extra moment condition
s is false, though it does not specify which ones are taken as just-identifying
 conditions.
 We simply conclude that the data does not support the statement that all
 moment conditions are correct.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, we are testing to see if if 
\begin_inset Formula $Z^{\prime}u\approx0$
\end_inset

.
 This corresponds to testing whether the minimand, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(\hat{\beta}_{GMM})$
\end_inset

, is close to zero or not.
 This is equivalent to regressing the 2SLS residuals on the instruments,
 and then testing whether 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}=0$
\end_inset

.
 The test statistic is distributed as 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}(\mbox{number of over-identifying restrictions})$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It should be noted that this test is severly limited due to the following
 reasons:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The null hypothesis has us assume that the instruments are valid in just-identif
ied models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It amounts to testing whether different instruments yield different estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Do this if the number of instruments is unwieldy (more informative).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What if we don't reject the null hypothesis? Well, this could reflect a
 lack of precision, or that the set of instruments could lead to similarly
 biased estimates.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What if we do reject the null hypothesis? Then, this might simply reflect
 heterogenous treatment effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Violations of the Exclusion Restriction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Violations of the exclusion restriction are especially problematic with
 a weak first-stage.
 Consider the following outcome equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\alpha+\rho T_{i}+\gamma z_{i}+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

and participating equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T_{i}=\pi_{0}+\pi_{1}z_{i}+u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

What if 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\neq0$
\end_inset

? Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\rho}_{IV} & = & \frac{\mbox{Cov}(\alpha+\rho T_{i}+\gamma z_{i}+\epsilon_{i},z_{i})}{\mbox{Cov}(T_{i},z_{i})}\\
 & = & \frac{\rho\mbox{Cov}(T_{i},z_{i})+\gamma\mbox{Cov}(z_{i},z_{i})}{\mbox{Cov}(T_{i},z_{i})}\\
 & = & \rho+\gamma\frac{\mbox{Var}(z_{i})}{\mbox{Cov}(T_{i},z_{i})}\\
 & = & \rho+\frac{\gamma}{\pi_{1}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, there is a larger bias when the first-stage is small.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Hausman Test or Durbin-Wu-Hausman Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This is a test for whether 2 different estimators have the same probability
 limit.
 We are often interested in comparing 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{OLS}$
\end_inset

 versus 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}$
\end_inset

.
 The null and alternative hypotheses are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
H_{0}:\mbox{p\lim}\ (\hat{\beta}-\tilde{\beta}=0)\\
H_{a}:\mbox{p\lim}\ (\hat{\beta}-\tilde{\beta}\neq0)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-consistency,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}-\tilde{\beta}\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N(0,V_{H})
\]

\end_inset

The test is difficult to assess because we do not know 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{H}$
\end_inset

.
 Yet, we do know
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N^{-1}V_{H}=V\left[\hat{\beta}-\tilde{\beta}\right]=V\left[\hat{\beta}\right]+V\left[\tilde{\beta}\right]-2\mbox{Cov}\left[\hat{\beta},\tilde{\beta}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Additional assumptions are required, or we can bootstrap.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Forbidden Regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we are interested in
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\alpha x_{i}+\rho T_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

We estimate by plugging in 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{T}_{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\alpha x_{i}+\rho\hat{T}_{i}+\left[\epsilon_{i}+\rho(T_{i}-\hat{T}_{i})\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By construction, the error term
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\epsilon_{i}+\rho(T_{i}-\hat{T}_{i})\right]
\]

\end_inset

is uncorrelated with the regressor, 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\hat{T}_{i}$
\end_inset

,
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
 if the regressor is based on a linear model.
 Therefore, do not estimate the first-stage regression with a non-linear
 model.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
III) Heterogeneous Treatment Effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given the usual IV assumption and monotonicity (i.e., everyone affected by
 the instrument is affected in the same direction), IV estimates a local
 average treatment effect (LATE).
 In regards to monotonicity, always takes, never takers, compliers are fine,
 but defiers are not.
 The monotonicity assumption means that people are not any less likely to
 accept treatment if they have been assigned it.
 To understand why we need the monotonicity assumption, note that the reduced-fo
rm effect of the instrument on outcomes may be zero because the effects
 on defiers cancels out the effects on compliers, even if the treatment
 effect is positive for all.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The local average treatment effect (LATE) is the average causual effect
 for the group that is affected by the instrument (i.e., the compliers).
 its usefulness depends on the nature of the study.
 In some sense, all estimates are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

local
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 because they are driven by where the variation lies, but that may not always
 be where we want it to lie.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
IV) IV/2SLS Estimates Can Be Imprecise
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Sometimes, IV and 2SLS estimates can be imprecise.
 What if the instrument is random, or condionally random? This does not
 mean the instrument is valid because it might affect outcomes through multiple
 channels.
 Though, we can obtain valid estimates of the reduced-from effect of the
 instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is exogenous, it is better to not use an instrument because 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}$
\end_inset

 explains less variation than 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
What To Include in an IV Paper
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Practical Matters
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Show the first-stage regression results with F-statistics.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Sometimes, you will even see partial 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{p}^{2}$
\end_inset

, which is a different way of approaching the same fundamental issue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Define 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 as the endogenous variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 as the instrument, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 as a vector of exogenous covariates.
 Purge 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 of the effects of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 by obtaining the residuals from a regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 (for 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{1}$
\end_inset

) and 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 (for 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{z}$
\end_inset

).
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{p}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is just the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{1}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{z}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{p}^{2}$
\end_inset

 tells us the share of the variation in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 that is captured by 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 after controlling for covariates.
 Obviously, a larger 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{p}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is better than a smaller one.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Make the case for the exclusion restriction rhetorically.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Recall the example from Assignment 4:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{TestScores\_G2}=\alpha+\beta\mbox{SmallClass\_G2}+\gamma_{s}+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

A possible IV is an indicator for whether or not students were initially
 assigned to a small class.
 If the determinants of the IV have any effect on test scores, then the
 exclusion restriction fails.
 In the field experiment, initial class was randomly assigned conditional
 on school of attendance, so we do not have to worry about this problem.
 However, if the IV affects test scores through any channels other than
 class assignment in the current grade, then the exclusion will still fail.
 Obviously, initial class assignment influences what type of class a student
 is assigned to prior to grade 2.
 In turn, these earlier class assignments will have an effect on test scores
 in grade 2.
 Thus, the exclusion restriction fails in this setup.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Test that the instrument is not related to observables we know to be related
 to outcomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Show reduced-form effects of the instrument on the outcome variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Verify the 2SLS are what we expect from (a) and (d).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
A Variable that is RA or Conditionally RA
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What if we have a variable that is RA or conditionally RA?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Question 1:
\series default
 Do we really believe only one mechanism is relevant, and this mechanism
 is of interest? If 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

yes,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then proceed with IV estimation.
 If no, then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Question 2: 
\series default
Will igoring other channels lead to estimates that are biased in a convenient
 way? If 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

yes,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then proceed with IV estimation.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Example*
We wish to determine the effects of military service on crime.
 Draft eligibility is used as an instrument for military service, but eligibilit
y may affect crime through education.
 Thus, using draft eligibility as an instrument for service will understate
 the effects of military service on crime, assuming that education reduces
 crime.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Question 3:
\series default
 Do we really have a variable that captures this mechanism? If 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

yes,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then proceed with IV estimation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the answer to either of the last two questions is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then is just the reduced-form effect itself of any interest? If the answer
 to this question is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then you should find a new project because there is no useful analysis
 to be done.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

On the other hand, if the answer is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

yes,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then we can estimate the reduced-form (RF) effect.
 You do so by acknowledging that the effects might be driven by other channels,
 such as 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

, etc.
 Then, explore the mechanisms by estimating the effecs on as many of these
 as you can.
 If you can explore the most relevant channels, and only one seems to matter,
 then you can provide an implied IV estimate with the appropriate caveats.
 The more credible the exclusion restriction, based on priors and what you
 observe, the more this IV estimate should be emphasized.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Reed Walker used the Clean Air Act (CAA) to measure the effect of pollution
 in early life on adult earnings.
 As stated, the quasi-experiment is provided by the CAA.
 The reduced-form effect is clearly of interest.
 The IV (i.e., the introduction of the CAA) is also of interest, but can we
 be sure that pollution is the only relevant channel? What about effects
 on local labor market conditions, or other early life circumstances? If
 the paper focuses on the reduced-form effect, then there is less to criticize.
 You can usually provide both IV and reduced-form estimates, but you must
 be careful on what you are emphasizing.
 So, Walker still provides IV estimates because the assumption is not too
 far-fetched.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Practical Matters For All Research Designs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Start with a parsimonious model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Sometimes it is even useful to analyze a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

wrong model,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which is believed to produce biased results, as a point of comparison,
 and then follow this with a Hausman test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One must show the most parsimonious model that is consistent with the thought
 experiment, e.g., Wald estimates for IV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If estimates are sensitive to the inclusion of additional controls, then
 this casts doubt on the validity of research design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Your 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

preferred model
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 might include controls to get more precise estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
If you argue 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is random, then the baseline specificaiton should not include controls.
 Also, if you argue that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 is random, conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

, then that should be the baseline specification.
 Moreover, estimated effects should not change with the inclusion of additional
 controls.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
A Brief Outline of IV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Basic Idea
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we want to estimate a treatment effect using observational data.
 The OLS estiamtor is biased and inconsistent due to correlation between
 the regressor and the error term.
 If a direct solution is not available, instrumental variables regression
 offers an alternative way to obtain a consistent estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As stated previously, IV estimation is used when the model has endogenous
 regressors.
 So, IV can be used to address the following important threats to internal
 validity:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Omitted variable bias from a variable that is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 but is not observed, so it cannot be included in the regression.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Simultaneous causality bias (endogenous explanatory variables; 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 causes 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 causes 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Measurement error of the regressors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instrumental variables regression can eliminate bias from these three sources.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the following equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Variation in the endogenous regressor, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, has two parts:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The part that is uncorrelated with the error term (i.e., the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

good
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The part that is correlated with the error term (i.e., the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bad
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The basic idea behind instrumental variables regression is to isolate the
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

good
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation and disregard the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bad
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation.
 The necessary conditions for a valid IV, 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

, are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Relevance:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Corr}(z_{i},x_{i})\neq0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Exogeneity:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Corr}(z_{i},\epsilon_{i})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It can be difficult to find an instrument that is both relevant and exogenous.
 Assessment of instrument exogeneity can be highly subjective when the model
 is just-identified.
\end_layout

