\begin_layout Subsection*
Testing Instrument Relevance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first-stage F-statistic testing the hypothesis that the coefficients
 on the instruments are jointly zero should be at least 10.
 A small F-statistic means the instruments are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

weak
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and the estimator is biased.
 If you have the same number of instruments and endogenous regressors, it
 is impossible to test for whether the instruments are exogenous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Overidentifying Restrictions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the model is over-identified, then we can perform a test of overidentifying
 restrictions.
 Regress the residuals from the 2SLS regression on the instruments and test
 whether the coefficients on the instruments are all zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Potential Pitfalls With IV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A bad instrument is one that is correlated with the omitted variables.
 Consequently, the bias of IV estimates is greater than OLS estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Example*
The weather in Brazil probably shifts the supply curve for coffee, providing
 a plausible instrument to estimate the effect of price on quantity demanded.
 Though, weather in Brazil might also shift the demand for coffee if sophisticat
ed commercial buyers at the NYC Coffee, Sugar, & Cocoa Exchange, where coffee
 futures are traded, use weather data to adjust holdings in anticipation
 of a price increase that may not in fact materialize.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The bias of instrumental variable estimates from very weak instruments tend
 to be centered on the corresponding OLS bias.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A nonlinear first-stage does not generate consistent estimates unless the
 nonlinear model happens to be exactly right.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Nonlinear second stage estimates with continuous or multivariated regressors
 are similarly tricky, requiring a correctly specified functional form in
 order to interpret the estimates easily.
 Even if the underlying second-stage relationship is nonlinear, linear IV
 estimates such as 2SLS typically capture an average effect of economic
 interest analogous to the LATE parameter for dummy endogenous regressors.
 2SLS is a robust estimation method that provides a natural starting point
 for IV applications.
\end_layout

